    "textSnapshot": "Problem: Brain retrieval ranks results by relevance alone. Older decisions and facts surface indefinitely even after newer ones supersede them, because the ranking system has no mechanism to express that fresher information should be preferred in near-tie scenarios.\nSolution: Introduced time-weighted ranking as an opt-in feature in retrieval. A new recency module applies a deterministic multiplier to fused scores that decays from 1.5× for fresh records to 0.3× for ancient ones, with a 90-day half-life. The multiplier:\nReorders results on near-ties without filtering or dropping anything\nNever overwhelms relevance (5× spread ensures stronger matches win regardless of age)\nLeaves undated records neutral (1.0×) rather than demoting them\nWorks only when explicitly enabled via retrievalOptions.Recency\nThe feature integrates into the ranking pipeline after RRF fusion. Fact records use UpdatedAt (when revised) rather than creation time, so edits naturally become current. History records carry no timestamp and are treated as neutral, preserving their rank. The default is off to maintain existing behavior for all current callers.",
